# THREE.JS / INTELLIGENCE CORE RULES

Applies to anything under `components/three/` and `components/animation/`.
See spec Sections 5A, 6, 22, 23, 27 for full detail.

## Single-core architecture

- There is ONE core system: `components/three/IntelligenceCore.tsx`, composed
  of `NodeNetwork.tsx`, `DataStream.tsx`, `CameraController.tsx`, and
  `GLogoParticles.tsx`.
- Sections do not each get their own 3D object. A section changes the core's
  *state* (target shape, node count, connection pattern, color emphasis),
  driven by scroll position. Before creating a new Three.js component, check
  whether the need can instead be a new state/prop on the existing core.
- `GLogoParticles.tsx` holds the G-shaped point cloud (sampled from the logo
  SVG path) used as one of the core's morph targets — this is what the
  particle system dissolves from / converges back into at the start and end
  of the homepage story.

## Performance (hard constraints, not suggestions)

- Keep draw calls and polygon count low; reuse materials; dispose unused
  resources on unmount.
- Lazy-load any GLB/texture assets; use compressed textures, Draco/Meshopt
  and KTX2 where applicable.
- Pause off-screen animation using `IntersectionObserver`; never animate a
  scene that isn't visible.
- Keep WebGL state outside the normal React render loop where it would
  otherwise cause unnecessary re-renders.
- Desktop target: 60 FPS where practical. Mobile target: 30–60 FPS.

## Mobile / reduced-motion

- Do not simply scale the desktop scene down. Build a deliberately lighter
  mobile composition: fewer particles, simpler geometry, no expensive
  shaders or post-processing, less continuous animation.
- The G ⇄ network signature loop must still exist on mobile in a simplified
  form — never silently remove it, only lighten it.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`: provide a static or minimally-animated
  fallback state for the core.

## Accessibility

- WebGL must never be the only carrier of important information. Anything
  meaningful shown in the 3D scene (headlines, section names, CTAs) must
  also exist as real semantic HTML.
